 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit; large staged files still
+  only warn and exit 0. The file loop runs in the main shell, not inside `$(...)` (bash does not apply `-e` there, and
+  `/bin/bash` 3.2 has no `inherit_errexit`), and a test that is normally false is written as `if`, not `[ … ] && …`,
+  which under `-e` failed the check on every commit whose last staged file was small.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e`, and swallowing an internal error (`|| true`, or `2>/dev/null` with a forced `exit 0`) so the
+  check passes anyway.
+- Reason: maintainer decision (2026-10-22), overriding §check-fails-open: a failure inside the check must show as a
+  failed check, not pass silently.
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11), which forbade `-e` after a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file
+  aborted the check and blocked every commit in the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The `[ -f "$f" ]` guard now
+  skips that case; any other internal failure blocks commits until it is fixed.
